@User_11 I’d like to tighten the scope a bit based on what Ops just found — the exception path is crossing two handoffs, so we should capture the owner and fallback step explicitly rather than treating it as a simple yes/no.  

That changes the original decision: **a narrow exception-owner check is no longer enough**, because Legal and Customer Success are reading the same exception differently. In Project Meridian, we avoided late sign-off churn by adding a short exception-handling block with **owner, backup approver, and downstream handoff steps** before review freeze.  

**Proposal:** replace the yes/no gate with a 3-line exception section covering owner, backup approver, and handoff sequence. That keeps the June 28 path clean and prevents ambiguity from resurfacing at sign-off.

We’re 22% into runbook prep, and the biggest gap I see is cutover ownership versus day-two support ownership. I’d push for a single RACI tied to the spec: http://sharepoint/treasury_management_system_implementation/operations_runbook_preparation_spec, then lock escalation paths and rollback decision points off that.  

Two options: keep refining team-by-team inputs, or freeze the core support model now and force any exceptions through one owner. I’m leaning to the second, because the open items around incident response, access provisioning, and business-hour coverage are becoming blockers, not details.  

@Treasury Operations @Infrastructure @Support — can you confirm by Friday which option you support and who owns each cutover task?

yep — that blocker is real, and i’d keep support from signing off until the handoff path is explicit.  
@infrastructure and @business ops, can you post owner, backup, escalation trigger, and rollback checkpoint in http://sharepoint/treasury_management_system_implementation/operations_runbook_preparation_spec by friday?  
once that lands, support can finalize day-one coverage off one clean version, and we can freeze the runbook section without guessing later.

Count me in on the working session — I’m a little uneasy that rollback and evidence mapping are still open.  
@Finance, @IT Ops, and @Support please post owner, path, and backup coverage in the spec by EOD Thursday: http://sharepoint/treasury_management_system_implementation/operations_runbook_preparation_spec.  
@Support, I also need the day-one service desk script updated with the revised cutover comms before we lock handoff; can we get that confirmed Friday?

Agreed — that SLA is the right operating boundary for July 6, because it matches actual support capacity.  
What still needs to land is the named after-hours escalation owner, since that’s the handoff Support will need when incidents occur outside business hours.  
@IT Operations please log the SLA and coverage window in http://sharepoint/treasury_management_system_implementation/operations_runbook_preparation_spec, and @Support/@TreasuryOps please post the after-hours owner by EOD so we can freeze this cleanly.

Yes — Security can own evidence cleanly only if IT Ops locks the publish-hold rule and backup approver.  
I’d put Security on retention/access and timestamp evidence, with the exact storage path and retention window captured in http://sharepoint/treasury_management_system_implementation/operations_runbook_preparation_spec.  
@IT Operations please add the publish-hold rule, backup approver, and timestamp fallback by EOD; @Security and @Finance validate the same version after that.

IT Ops can stamp the backup approver and re-sign-off trigger today; that keeps the gate clean.  
For coverage hours, I’d set 24/7 for go-live week with a named delegate and 1-business-hour response SLA, then have @Security and @Infra confirm the delegate in the spec today.  
@IT Operations please update http://sharepoint/treasury_management_system_implementation/operations_runbook_preparation_spec by EOD, and can @Security / @Infra post the named delegate so we can freeze this path?

Agreed — the cleanest path is to close rollback, handoff checkpoints, and evidence validation in one pass.  
@Security @Infrastructure @Support please confirm your procedure owners and any gaps in http://sharepoint/treasury_management_system_implementation/operations_runbook_preparation_spec by EOD today.  

If those three land now, IT Ops can freeze the operational sections and we keep July 6 on track — can we lock that in?

Let’s freeze that operational slice today; Meridian taught me “almost confirmed” becomes day-one pain fast.  
@Security, @Infra, and @Support should stamp owners in http://sharepoint/treasury_management_system_implementation/operations_runbook_preparation_spec by EOD, then IT Ops can lock rollback and handoff sections.  
Should I be worried that any backup approver is still unnamed, or are we clean to close this?

Yes — that’s the risk pattern I’m watching too, and different versions of the support model are not safe at 86%.  

@IT Operations please publish one final escalation path in http://sharepoint/treasury_management_system_implementation/operations_runbook_preparation_spec today, and @TreasuryOps/@ServiceDesk/@Infra confirm they’re aligned on that version by EOD.  

If those confirmations land, we freeze the operational sections and keep validation checkpoints as the only remaining sign-off item; can we lock this in?

@Legal @HR @Operations, send the top 5 exception scenarios by Friday.  
I’ll map them to plain-language cues and control expectations, then lock the first draft.  

One ask: flag any policy wording changes now, before the rollout plan gets baked in.

We’re only 4% into Model Feature Engineering, so I want to lock the kickoff path now. The biggest risk is building strong-looking features that later clash with alert tuning or investigation workflows, so I’m pushing for early validation on the highest-value items first.  

I’ve linked the working spec here: http://sharepoint/aml_(anti-money_laundering)_project/model_feature_engineering_spec. @Data Engineering and @Compliance, please confirm any data gaps, segment caveats, or policy constraints by Friday so we can avoid rework.  

@Modeling team, please come ready with your top five candidate features, expected lift, and any downstream dependencies for a 30-minute review this week. Can we lock that meeting and get owners assigned today?

Absolutely aligned—on Cedar, a missing backup commander turned a clean dry run into a two-hour scramble.  

@ProjectManager, please lock backup contacts, incident commander backup, and rollback authority in the spec today.  

Should I be worried that access approvals and bridge staffing are still drifting?

Agreed — 30 minutes is enough if we keep it tight on rules, evidence, and handoffs.  
@Compliance @Operations @Investigations please post open questions in-thread today, then let’s book the working session this week and use the spec as the single source: http://sharepoint/aml_(anti-money_laundering)_project/investigation_workflow_definition_spec.  
I’d push for a named owner on escalation, evidence capture, and fallback SLA before build starts — can we lock that in?

Engineering needs to answer this explicitly: discrete build fields for effective date, citation, and owner, or log-only.  
If it’s log-only, are we sure downstream validation and audit evidence won’t drift again?  
@engineering please confirm by Friday, and @compliance/@legal should name the fallback if the build can’t store them.

Full dry run is the right move; I’ve seen “light validation” become a feature-length disaster on Project Aurora.  
@ProjectManager, please freeze the spec today, and let’s get primary/backup coverage plus access approvals signed off by EOD Friday.  
If Security or Infrastructure still has loose ends, call them now—production loves a plot twist.

Yes, DE should put the backup owner in SharePoint too, not just the primary cutoff owner.  
I think we also need the record location, approval timestamp, and reason code in the same entry, so audit doesn’t split later.  
@Data Engineering can you post that bundle by Friday, and can @Model Risk confirm the approver name in writing?

Send them exactly that way: UTC cutoff, owner, source link, and evidence hash per item.  
Anything missing source linkage or a clean timestamp is a blocker, not a handoff.  
@User_7, post the Ops list and I’ll separate remediation-only from true gaps fast.

Agreed — that broader tag set is the right filter, because ownership and evidence gaps usually surface later in testing.  
@Treasury please tag bank connectivity and approval-chain items in the spec as mandatory control, clarification, or new scope, with owner, decision date, and impacted control/test artifact by EOD today: http://sharepoint/treasury_management_system_implementation/risk:_stakeholder_scope_gaps_spec  
Can you also confirm whether the approval-chain evidence lives in the TMS log, an archive, or both?

I’m comfortable locking the ownership split now, but only if Legal/Ops confirm the clause taxonomy first.  

The one possible snag is whether “reporting” and “retention” are treated as control-impacting or just implementation notes; if we blur that, the log becomes cosmetic.  

@User_10 owner, @User_13 backup works for me, with clause ID, timestamp, change-summary, and Legal re-read mandatory before Ops touches http://sharepoint/regulatory_compliance_program/draft_compliance_policies_spec.

Yes — Vendor needs to map cutoff, weekend/holiday behavior, rerun ownership, and the missed-drop approval path in the spec.  
I want that captured as baseline control behavior, not an ops afterthought, against http://sharepoint/treasury_management_system_implementation/risk:_vendor_integration_complexity_spec.  
@Vendor, post the process and owner by Friday; @Security, confirm if manual approval is mandatory for missed-window reruns.

I’m aligned on the review ask, and the boundary needs to stay tight: cash positioning, bank connectivity, and reporting owners should flag only true scope gaps.  

@Business @Technical @Test — please post unresolved decisions and dependency owners in http://sharepoint/treasury_management_system_implementation/risk:_stakeholder_scope_gaps_spec by Friday, especially anything that could move July 18.  

If Treasury can separate “in-scope now” from “design detail later,” PMO can freeze the baseline cleanly. Can we lock that in?

Agreed — blocker type belongs on every open row, not just critical ones, so immediate review stays clean.  
@Compliance owns the register, and @Ops should add blocker type, owner, ETA, and provisional reason by EOD Wednesday against http://sharepoint/aml_(anti_money_laundering)_project/risk:_incomplete_regulatory_coverage_spec.  
That keeps Friday’s cut decision-ready and avoids false-green. Can @Ops confirm the full row format today so we can lock it in?

@compliance needs to name the threshold owner and approver today, otherwise we’re still blocked.  
@engineering, can you confirm owner/timestamp/effective date are discrete fields by Friday, or is signed fallback log the only option?  
Can we get that split resolved in-thread now?

Agreed on the one-owner rule.  
@Ops and @Data, please drop the exact names in http://sharepoint/aml_(anti-money_laundering)_project/investigation_workflow_definition_spec today.  

Can @Compliance confirm the escalation criteria wording after that?

Yes, I’d keep priority jurisdictions to highest-volume plus recent regulatory-change corridors first.  
@Compliance can you confirm that cut, and @Model can you sign off on the staged expansion?  
@Ops should keep lower-risk lanes visible in http://sharepoint/aml_(anti-money_laundering)_project/risk:_incomplete_regulatory_coverage_spec until Data clears the backlog—can we lock that split today?

Agreed—Security should name one approver, one evidence type, and the audit trail owner by Friday.  
That keeps API freeze from outpacing control ownership, which is where rework usually starts.  
@Security, can you post the lean chain in http://sharepoint/treasury_management_system_implementation/risk:_vendor_integration_complexity_spec and confirm whether any backup approver is required?

Finance owns the fallback, and @IT should confirm bank-response validation by EOD Friday.  
Keep owner, defect ID, and retest date locked in the log now.  
@User_12 can you update the spec and confirm the audit packet status today?

I’d lean stricter too, but only if @Compliance confirms the new control language by EOD.  
@Data Engineering, can you post the impacted rule rows and source mappings today?  
Can @Legal and @Ops reply yes/no on the updated interpretation so we lock scope?

Yes — Legal/Risk can tag the high-risk items first, and I think that should stop the register from stalling.  

For the single owner, can @Compliance take it and keep the cutoff moving against http://sharepoint/aml_(anti-money_laundering)_project/risk:_incomplete_regulatory_coverage_spec?  

@Ops, can you send the first cut with high-risk flagged by Wednesday, then @Risk adds expiry after the high-risk list is locked?

@ProjectManager I’m thinking we may need to adjust the plan slightly based on the coverage gaps we just found—  

The original call to proceed with a full dry run only after backup bridge coverage, rollback authority, and evidence ownership were frozen in the spec no longer holds. On the latest runbook validation, the named backup bridge owner is unavailable on July 19, and two evidence owners have conflicting obligations during the dry-run window.  

**Proposed change:** run a smaller controlled simulation with the available core owners now, and reschedule full-run approval until backup coverage and evidence ownership are fully staffed. I saw the same failure mode on Project Bellwether: we pushed a full rehearsal without complete audit coverage, and the handoff gaps showed up exactly where we least wanted them.

@legal @compliance @engineering Given the new guidance check and Legal’s open question, I’d suggest we pause the full template post for now and use a short interim checklist so we don’t hard-code the wrong fields.

**Change to the prior decision:** don’t post the immutable decision log template today. Compliance found the minimum evidence set is still missing the required exception-handling traceability field, and Legal needs to confirm citation/threshold language first.

**New path:** Legal confirms citation + threshold interpretation, Compliance posts a temporary minimum evidence checklist in-thread, then we publish the final immutable template once fields are locked. This keeps the control accurate and avoids rework later!

@Legal @HR @Operations Given the updated Legal guidance and the LMS rework risk, I think we should shift from a hard Friday lock to a **soft freeze** on core content and push **final sign-off to the next business day** after the revised copy is validated.  

The original Friday cutoff now creates avoidable churn in the training deck, manager briefing, and rollout pack—basically a compliance edition of “The Groundhog Day.”  
**New plan:** core scenarios and owner/action matrix stay frozen now; Legal confirms updated wording; downstream teams validate impact; then we close final sign-off once those checks are done.

@Security @Vendor I’d tweak the timing a bit based on what Security just surfaced: if the audit trail owner has to be explicit now, we should lock that with the API baseline and only leave the downstream lineage/SOX assignments provisional for the moment.  

The original plan to freeze only API standard and error handling, then name ownership later, leaves a control gap; Vendor’s non-standard field also depends on Finance’s reporting owner, so delaying all ownership creates avoidable rework next cycle.  

**New baseline:** freeze API standard, error handling, and audit trail owner now; defer only final lineage and SOX ownership until the payload mapping exception is closed.

@User_3 Given the repeat failures across interfaces, I think we should change course and hold these fixes for one coordinated remediation wave before retesting.  

QA found the same mapping defect reappearing in three interfaces, and bank-response validation is drifting by file format; in Orion, that pattern meant isolated retests just burned time.  
**New plan:** batch all payment-format, mapping, and bank-response defects, merge once, then run one full-path retest with Finance as fallback owner.

@User_13 Given the late Legal/Risk input, I think we should pause the hard freeze for one more day and keep Reporting evidence-only until the written sign-off lands.  
**What changed:** Legal’s note leaves one threshold needing a final jurisdiction check, and Risk can’t sign off until tomorrow morning because they still need to validate the evidence owner against the updated spec. On **Harborgate**, we froze too early on a stale threshold set, and the cleanup cost us a full rework cycle.  
**Proposal:** keep the exception log live, hold the freeze in Compliance, and target a one-day slip in the baseline lock so we don’t harden the wrong control state.

@User_6 I think we should tighten the scope a bit based on what we’re seeing in validation: the original “soft ambiguity = review-trigger, hard-stop = legal/threshold failure” split is too broad for the current state.  

Engineering found inconsistent jurisdiction/entity-type mappings because downstream KYC responses don’t reliably separate **“unknown”** from **“not applicable”**, and Compliance also flagged that blessing every mixed case now would push the July 17 handoff.  

So here’s the better path: keep **soft ambiguity** as a temporary triage label, but only formalize **hard-stop** and **review-trigger** rules for the **tested entity-type/jurisdiction pairs**; route all other mixed cases to **manual Compliance review** until the response semantics are stable.

@User_7 Given the spot-check findings, I think we should tighten the closure rule a bit and require a versioned artifact check before we mark provisional controls closed.  

The old rule — reporting-ready closes once Ops confirms the evidence source, with only reviewer-proof items going to IA — is no longer safe. Ops found the source was correct, but the linked artifact had been replaced after review, so the current matrix fields still allowed a false close.  

**New rule:** keep reporting-ready as an internal status only, and require provisional controls to have **source, timestamp, versioned artifact link, owner, and reviewer-proof-ready** before closure. IA stays limited to exceptions where the artifact trail is incomplete or the primary control is genuinely broken.

@User_1 Given the jurisdiction-specific confirmation and the live wording drift Reporting found, I think we should shift from draft-only to a controlled working baseline so we can lock the approved clauses and manage exceptions explicitly.  

The original draft-only decision is no longer viable: Legal confirmed “mandatory vs. optional” varies by jurisdiction, and Reporting already found two live customer-facing docs using the lighter clause set. **New proposal:** move the matrix to a controlled working version now, with Legal-approved baseline clauses, Reporting-owned customer-facing variants, and jurisdictional overrides tagged with owner, clause, and expiry.  

That gets us out of policy limbo and into something auditable—less “choose your own adventure,” more “this is the canon.”

@User_3 I’d still keep us focused, but I think we need to widen the approval slightly now because the mobile test surfaced a real gap in the verification recovery path that could create rework if we ignore it.  

In “Northstar,” we approved the happy path too early, and the device-switch edge case became a support fire drill later. **New ask:** include the failed-SMS-twice → switch-device flow, plus recovery states, in prototype approval before sign-off.  

The data suggests this isn’t optional cleanup; it’s a meaningful drop-off path, so a small delay now beats redesign churn in July.

@Finance Ops @IT @QA Quick update — the bank dry run exposed a traceability issue on exception paths, so we should do a targeted control checkpoint before freeze rather than lock the current pattern today.  

**New finding:** approval audit fields populate inconsistently when a transaction routes to exception handling, and Finance + QA confirmed it would break recon traceability if frozen now.  
**Change:** hold a short checkpoint on exception routing, audit field population, and reporting dependencies, then freeze the baseline once those controls are verified against http://sharepoint/treasury_management_system_implementation/cash_management_module_build_spec.

Finance owning fallback is right; we used that split in Prism, and it saved a broken UAT handoff.  
I’d still worry if the audit packet isn’t updated today—owner, defect ID, and retest date need to match the log exactly.  
@User_12, please update the spec and confirm the packet status in SharePoint by EOD.

@Data Engineering Given the validation findings from DE, I think we should adjust the plan slightly and keep Phase 2 as a placeholder in the spec for now rather than freezing the exclusion path today.  
**Why:** unresolved source mappings + inconsistent reason codes in two high-risk segments mean the exclusion logic isn’t stable enough to lock.  

**Action:** keep Phase 2 tracked in http://sharepoint/aml_(anti_money_laundering)_project/model_feature_engineering_spec with open issues documented, then define the exclusion path after source mapping and reason-code review are complete.

@user_4 given the daylight-saving mismatch we just found, i think we should slightly adjust the approach rather than hard-lock everything right now—  

on “meridian,” we got burned when a cutoff bug made valid backfills look like drift, and we had to unwind a bad quarantine later. **new plan:** quarantine only rows with unresolved timezone mismatch, and let late-arrival rows with tested fallback + finance-approved cutoff logic flow into a controlled staging set for revalidation.  

that keeps true drift blocked, but stops us from freezing legit backfills into the wrong bucket.

Yes, please tag the branch owner per exception type, or we may get three triage habits.  
I’d map nulls to hard-stop, partial-match to fast-track, and overwritten to verified-override unless Compliance says otherwise.  
@Ops can you publish that branch-to-rule table by Friday, and @Compliance confirm if any branch needs a different evidence bundle?

Treasury taught me that parked items only stay parked when the tags bite back.  
I’d keep approver and re-open trigger mandatory, then make retention-rule mandatory only when evidence timing or immutability changes—otherwise the intake queue turns into a sequel nobody asked for.  

@Finance can name the decision owner by Friday, and @Audit should review only the approval-timing items that alter evidence capture or sign-off traceability. Can we codify that threshold in the spec so UAT doesn’t inherit zombie exceptions?

Yep, that interim checklist path feels safer to me too 😬  
If @legal can lock the citation and threshold wording today, I think we can keep validation moving without guessing.  

@engineering, could you confirm by Thursday whether owner and effective date are discrete fields or still log-only? I’m a little worried we’ll miss a dependency if that answer slips.

Yes — I’d treat that as a real risk, especially for newer high-risk transaction types and any jurisdiction-specific thresholds!  
@Compliance @Ops @Data, please do a fast edge-case sweep against http://sharepoint/aml_(anti-money_laundering)_project/risk:_incomplete_regulatory_coverage_spec and flag anything that still needs a redesign pass by EOD Thursday.  
If the gaps are just scenario coverage, keep rollout moving; if they affect alerting or feed logic, we need them marked as blockers right away!

I’m aligned to freeze the branch model now; it keeps the audit trail clean and avoids another rework loop.  
@Operations please post the final handoff rule and named owners in the spec by EOD Thursday, then @Reporting mirror verbatim.  
If those land, can @Compliance give the same-day sign-off so we can close this phase?

Yes — I think “disposition logged” should include timestamp, user, and reason code.  
@Ops, can you confirm the exact trigger field and what the fallback is if the timer source drops out?  
If those three audit fields are mandatory, I’m okay freezing that in http://sharepoint/aml_(anti-money_laundering)_project/investigation_workflow_definition_spec by Friday.

Ops owns the Friday package, and I think the handoff owners should be explicit per branch: null, partial-match, overwritten.  
@DataOwner, can you post the 3x3 tags by Friday with first-bad-system and branch tag?  
@Compliance, please confirm whether migration gets its own rule or stays under partial-match in http://sharepoint/aml_(anti-money_laundering)_project/risk:_inconsistent_kyc_records_spec.

Yes — branch tags should be mandatory for migration and partial-match, or Ops will misroute clean cases.  
I’d keep the override bundle lean: source system, timestamp, approver ID, reason, plus branch tag only where routing risk exists.  
@Ops can you publish that branch-to-rule table by Friday, and @Compliance confirm the migration rule in http://sharepoint/aml_(anti_money_laundering)_project/risk:_inconsistent_kyc_records_spec?

On Project Bellwether, day-one escalation failed because coverage existed, but routing didn’t.  
@IT @TreasuryOps @ServiceDesk, please name one primary escalation owner, one backup, and the exact handoff timestamp by EOD tomorrow in the spec: http://sharepoint/treasury_management_system_implementation/production_deployment_readiness_spec

Should I be worried that access failures and transaction issues still share an undefined triage path?

Agreed on the 3-branch publish, and yes, handoff owners need to be named in-thread.  
@Ops, please confirm ownership for null, partial-match, overwritten, and add migration as a sub-path or distinct rule in http://sharepoint/aml_(anti_money_laundering)_project/risk:_inconsistent_kyc_records_spec.  
@DataOwner, can you post the 3x3 tagged set by Friday with first-bad-system and branch tag?

@Compliance, can you post the outstanding rules list and local-market exceptions by EOD?  
@Operations and @Data, please add owner per remediation item, plus whether each gap hits alerting, routing, or escalation.  
Once that’s in the spec, can we freeze the fix plan and keep 2025-07-16 protected?

AML taught me the same trick in Cedar: freeze the core pack, then give late fixes a labeled lane so Legal doesn’t get a mystery box.  
I’d keep `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ`, owner, and evidence link mandatory at intake, and let missing-hash items ride as pending verification only if the link is clean.  

@User_7, can you drop the open Ops items into that queue today?

KYC mismatches are still blocking CDD validation, and we’re only 39% through this phase.  

I think we need a decision on the source-of-truth rule: option A, accept source system priority; option B, require manual Ops review on any conflict; option C, add a hard fail until Data fixes the record.  

@Operations @Data @Compliance — which option should we lock for this phase, and who owns the fix path by 2025-07-17?

Yep, I’m aligned on named handoff owners in-thread, because otherwise I’m a bit worried the routing will drift again 😬.  
@Ops, could you confirm whether migration is its own rule or a sub-path under partial-match in http://sharepoint/aml_(anti-money_laundering)_project/risk:_inconsistent_kyc_records_spec?  
@DataOwner, Friday for the 3x3 tagged set sounds workable, and I’d really like first-bad-system plus branch tag on every example so we’re not guessing later.

